{
 "id": "lem-av-tate-transition-surjective",
 "kind": "statement",
 "title": "The transition maps of the Tate module are surjective",
 "statement": "Let $A$ be an abelian variety over a field $k$ with algebraic closure $\\bar k$, and let\n$\\ell>0$ and $n\\ge0$ be integers. Then multiplication by $\\ell$ maps\n$A[\\ell^{n+1}](\\bar k)$ onto $A[\\ell^n](\\bar k)$.",
 "source": {
  "document": "literature/blueprint/main-20261028/blueprint/sec-abelian-varieties/sk-prop-av-tate-rank/lem-av-tate-transition-surjective.md",
  "place": "node lem-av-tate-transition-surjective, order 545"
 },
 "stmt_deps": [
  "def-sc-mult-n"
 ],
 "proof": [
  {
   "text": "Let $b\\in A[\\ell^n](\\bar k)$. The morphism $[\\ell]$ is finite and surjective\n([lem-sc-isogeny-facts], [lem-sc-mult-n-isogeny]), so $b$, viewed as a $\\bar k$-point\nof $A$, lifts to a $\\bar k$-point $a$ of $A$ with $\\ell a=b$\n([lem-sc-finite-flat-surj-points]). Then $\\ell^{n+1}a=\\ell^nb=0$, so\n$a\\in A[\\ell^{n+1}](\\bar k)$.",
   "uses": [
    "lem-sc-isogeny-facts",
    "lem-sc-mult-n-isogeny",
    "lem-sc-finite-flat-surj-points"
   ],
   "elementary": false
  }
 ],
